The `allowedUrls: ['*']` setting is a **critical security vulnerability** that completely defeats the purpose of URL restrictions. Here's why this is dangerous and how to fix it:

## Why `allowedUrls: ['*']` is Wrong

**Security risks:**
- ✅ Anyone can use your token on any website
- ✅ Malicious sites can embed your token and consume your API quota
- ✅ Your token can be harvested and used for unauthorized projects
- ✅ No protection if the token is accidentally exposed

**Real-world impact:**
```javascript
// With allowedUrls: ['*'], this works from ANY domain:
// - https://malicious-site.com
// - https://competitor.com  
// - http://localhost:8080 (anyone's localhost)
// - Any GitHub Pages site, CodePen, etc.

// Attacker can embed your token and drain your usage
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  accessToken: 'pk.YOUR_TOKEN_HERE' // Works everywhere!
});
```

## Correct URL Restrictions by Environment

### Option 1: Separate Tokens per Environment ✅ **RECOMMENDED**

Create different tokens for each environment:

**Production Token:**
```json
{
  "note": "Production - myapp.com",
  "scopes": ["styles:read", "fonts:read", "styles:tiles"],
  "allowedUrls": [
    "https://myapp.com/*",
    "https://www.myapp.com/*"
  ]
}
```

**Staging Token:**
```json
{
  "note": "Staging - staging.myapp.com", 
  "scopes": ["styles:read", "fonts:read", "styles:tiles"],
  "allowedUrls": [
    "https://staging.myapp.com/*",
    "https://dev.myapp.com/*"
  ]
}
```

**Development Token:**
```json
{
  "note": "Local Development",
  "scopes": ["styles:read", "fonts:read", "styles:tiles"],
  "allowedUrls": [
    "http://localhost:*",
    "http://127.0.0.1:*",
    "http://0.0.0.0:*"
  ]
}
```

### Option 2: Single Token (Less Secure)

If you must use one token, combine all environments:

```json
{
  "note": "All environments - myapp.com",
  "scopes": ["styles:read", "fonts:read", "styles:tiles"],
  "allowedUrls": [
    "https://myapp.com/*",
    "https://www.myapp.com/*",
    "https://staging.myapp.com/*", 
    "http://localhost:*",
    "http://127.0.0.1:*"
  ]
}
```

## Implementation Strategy

### 1. Environment-Based Token Selection

```javascript
// config.js
const getMapboxToken = () => {
  const hostname = window.location.hostname;

  if (hostname === 'myapp.com' || hostname === 'www.myapp.com') {
    return 'pk.PRODUCTION_TOKEN_HERE';
  } else if (hostname.includes('staging.myapp.com')) {
    return 'pk.STAGING_TOKEN_HERE';  
  } else if (hostname === 'localhost' || hostname === '127.0.0.1') {
    return 'pk.DEVELOPMENT_TOKEN_HERE';
  } else {
    console.error('Unknown environment:', hostname);
    return null;
  }
};

// Usage
mapboxgl.accessToken = getMapboxToken();
```

### 2. Build-Time Token Selection

```javascript
// webpack.config.js or similar
const MAPBOX_TOKENS = {
  production: 'pk.PRODUCTION_TOKEN_HERE',
  staging: 'pk.STAGING_TOKEN_HERE', 
  development: 'pk.DEVELOPMENT_TOKEN_HERE'
};

module.exports = {
  // ...
  plugins: [
    new webpack.DefinePlugin({
      'process.env.MAPBOX_TOKEN': JSON.stringify(
        MAPBOX_TOKENS[process.env.NODE_ENV]
      )
    })
  ]
};
```

## Migration Steps

**Immediate action:**
1. Create production token with proper URL restrictions
2. Deploy to production with new token
3. Test thoroughly
4. Revoke the unrestricted token

**For your development workflow:**
```bash
# Create separate development token  
# Add to local .env file (never commit!)
echo "MAPBOX_DEV_TOKEN=pk.YOUR_DEV_TOKEN_HERE" >> .env.local

# Update .gitignore
echo ".env.local" >> .gitignore
```

## Pro Tips

**URL Pattern Best Practices:**
- Use `https://` for production (never `http://`)
- Include both `myapp.com` and `www.myapp.com`
- Use `/*` to match all paths under a domain
- Be specific - don't use wildcards like `*.com`

**Security monitoring:**
- Check Mapbox dashboard for unexpected usage patterns
- Set up alerts for high API usage
- Regularly audit your active tokens

The key principle: **restrict first, open later if needed** rather than starting wide open. Your future self (and security team) will thank you!

**Symbol layers** are the right fix here. Let me explain why and show you the performance difference.

## The Problem with HTML Markers at Scale

Your current implementation probably looks like this:

```javascript
// ❌ What's killing performance: 75,000 DOM elements
stores.forEach((store) => {
  new mapboxgl.Marker()
    .setLngLat([store.lng, store.lat])
    .setPopup(new mapboxgl.Popup().setHTML(store.name))
    .addTo(map);
});
```

**Result:** 75,000 DOM elements, ~10+ second render time, completely unusable pan/zoom.

## Symbol Layers: The Right Solution

```javascript
// ✅ GPU-accelerated rendering, smooth at 75K+ features
map.addSource('stores', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: {
    type: 'FeatureCollection',
    features: stores.map(store => ({
      type: 'Feature',
      geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [store.lng, store.lat] },
      properties: { 
        name: store.name, 
        type: store.type,
        id: store.id 
      }
    }))
  }
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'stores',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'stores',
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'store-icon', // You'll need to add this to the map
    'icon-size': 0.6,
    'icon-allow-overlap': true, // Important for dense datasets
    'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
    'text-size': 11,
    'text-offset': [0, 1.2],
    'text-anchor': 'top',
    'text-optional': true // Text can be omitted if it would collide
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#333',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});

// Single click handler for all 75K stores
map.on('click', 'stores', (e) => {
  const store = e.features[0].properties;
  new mapboxgl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<h3>${store.name}</h3><p>Type: ${store.type}</p>`)
    .addTo(map);
});
```

**Performance:** 75,000 stores render in ~200ms, 60 FPS pan/zoom.

## Why Not Clustering?

Clustering is useful when you have **too many overlapping points** that create visual clutter. But for store locations:

1. **Users want to see individual stores** - they're looking for "the Starbucks near 5th St", not "there are 12 coffee shops in this area"
2. **Store density varies greatly** - clustering works poorly when you have sparse rural areas mixed with dense urban centers
3. **Zoom-dependent behavior is confusing** - stores appear/disappear as you zoom, making it hard to find specific locations

## When You WOULD Use Clustering

Clustering makes sense for:
- **Crime incidents** (patterns matter more than individual events)
- **Social media posts** (volume trends, not specific posts)
- **Sensor readings** (density patterns, not individual sensors)

## Production-Ready Symbol Layer Implementation

Here's what you'll need for 75K stores:

```javascript
// Add store icon to the map first
map.loadImage('/icons/store-marker.png', (error, image) => {
  if (error) throw error;
  map.addImage('store-icon', image);

  // Then add the layer
  addStoresLayer();
});

function addStoresLayer() {
  map.addSource('stores', {
    type: 'geojson', 
    data: storesGeoJSON,
    // Crucial for feature state and click handlers
    generateId: true
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'stores',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'stores',
    layout: {
      'icon-image': 'store-icon',
      'icon-size': [
        'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
        8, 0.4,  // Smaller at low zoom
        12, 0.6, // Medium at mid zoom  
        16, 0.8  // Larger at high zoom
      ],
      'icon-allow-overlap': true,
      // Show text only at higher zoom levels to reduce clutter
      'text-field': [
        'case',
        ['>', ['zoom'], 12], ['get', 'name'],
        '' // No text at low zoom
      ],
      'text-size': 11,
      'text-offset': [0, 1.5],
      'text-anchor': 'top',
      'text-optional': true
    },
    paint: {
      'text-color': '#333',
      'text-halo-color': '#fff',
      'text-halo-width': 1
    }
  });

  // Hover effects using feature state (efficient)
  let hoveredStoreId = null;

  map.on('mouseenter', 'stores', (e) => {
    map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer';

    if (hoveredStoreId !== null) {
      map.setFeatureState(
        { source: 'stores', id: hoveredStoreId }, 
        { hover: false }
      );
    }

    hoveredStoreId = e.features[0].id;
    map.setFeatureState(
      { source: 'stores', id: hoveredStoreId }, 
      { hover: true }
    );
  });

  map.on('mouseleave', 'stores', () => {
    map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '';

    if (hoveredStoreId !== null) {
      map.setFeatureState(
        { source: 'stores', id: hoveredStoreId }, 
        { hover: false }
      );
    }
    hoveredStoreId = null;
  });
}

// Update the symbol paint to use hover state
// Add this to the paint object:
'icon-opacity': [
  'case',
  ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
  1.0,   // Full opacity when hovered
  0.8    // Slightly transparent otherwise  
]
```

## Expected Performance Results

- **Render time:** ~200ms for initial 75K stores
- **Pan/zoom:** Smooth 60 FPS
- **Memory usage:** ~50 MB vs 500+ MB for HTML markers
- **Click response:** Instant (single event handler vs 75K handlers)

**Bottom line:** Symbol layers will solve your performance problem immediately. Save clustering for use cases where you actually want to show density patterns rather than individual locations.

